<span id="Description"></span>
==Beschreibung==

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
The [[Image:CfdOF_FluidMaterial.svg|24px]] '''CfdOF FluidMaterial''' command opens a task panel to edit a Fluid Properties‏‎ object. This object holds the properties of a fluid used in a simulation. These can be entered manually, or selected from a fluid library. One Fluid Properties‏‎ object is automatically added when an [[Image:CfdOF_Analysis.svg|16px]] [[CfdOF_Analysis|Analysis container]] is created. In case of a multi-phase [[CfdOF_PhysicsModel|Physics Model]], additional fluids can be defined by adding more Fluid Properties‏‎ objects.
</div>

<span id="Usage"></span>
==Anwendung==

<span id="Create_additional_fluid_data_object_(multi-phase)"></span>
===Zusätzliches Fluid-Datenobjekt erstellen (mehrphasig)===

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
# If required: activate the correct [[Image:CfdOF_Analysis.svg|16px]] [[CfdOF_Analysis|Analysis container]] by double-clicking it in the [[Tree_View|Tree View]].
# Ensure the {{PropertyData|Phase}} property of the [[Image:CfdOF_PhysicsModel.svg|16px]] [[CfdOF_PhysicsModel|Physics Model]] in the container is set to {{Value|FreeSurface}}.
# There are several ways to invoke the command:
#* Press the {{Button|[[Image:CfdOF_FluidMaterial.svg|16px]] [[CfdOF_FluidMaterial|Add fluid properties‏‎]]}} button.
#* Select the {{MenuCommand|CfdOF → [[Image:CfdOF_FluidMaterial.svg|16px]] Add fluid properties‏‎}} option from the menu.
# A [[Image:CfdOF_FluidMaterial.svg|16px]] '''FluidProperties''' data object is added to the container.
# The task panel opens. See [[#Options|Options]] for more information.
# Press the {{Button|OK}} button to confirm and finish the command.
</div>

<span id="Edit_fluid_data_object"></span>
===Fluid-Datenobjekt bearbeiten===

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
# Do one of the following:
#* Only for single phase: Follow steps 1 and 3 described above.
#* For single phase and multi-phase: Double-click a [[Image:CfdOF_FluidMaterial.svg|16px]] '''FluidProperties''' data object in the Tree View.
# The task panel opens. See [[#Options|Options]] for more information.
# Press the {{Button|OK}} button to confirm and finish the command.
</div>

<span id="Options"></span>
==Optionen==

Die im [[CfdOF_PhysicsModel/de|Physikmodell]] ausgewählten Optionen bestimmen, welche Eingaben im Aufgaben-Fenster Fluideigenschaften erforderlich sind. Die folgende Abbildung zeigt das Aufgaben-Fenster Fluideigenschaften für eine nicht-isotherme Simulation. 

[[File:CfdOF_DialogFluidProperties.png|393px|frameless|center|alt=Aufgaben-Fenster CfdOF-Eigenschaften|Aufgaben-Fenster CfdOF-Eigenschaften]]

<span id="Material_name"></span>
===Materialname===

In das Eingabefeld kann der Name der Flüssigkeit eingegeben werden. Eine aus der Bibliothek Vordefinierte Flüssigkeiten ausgewählte Flüssigkeit wird automatisch in dieses Eingabefeld übernommen, kann jedoch überschrieben werden. Der eingegebene Name erscheint in der Baumstruktur des [[CfdOF Analysis/de|Analyse-Containers]].

<span id="Fluid_properties"></span>
===Eigenschaften von Flüssigkeiten===

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
* Isothermal fluids
: The physical properties of an Isothermal fluid will not change during the simulation and therefore only data for a single temperature point is required.
</div>  

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
:* Input box '''Cp'''.
:: Specific Heat Capacity at constant pressure.
:: This is required when simulating non-isothermal, single phase fluid, under Steady State. Cp is the amount of energy required to raise the temperature of 1 kg of fluid by one degree Kelvin, in an isobaric process. As this is under Steady State, only a single data point is required at the conditions that the simulation is occurring.
</div> 

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
:* Input box '''Density'''.
::Mass per unit of volume of a material/fluid.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
:* Input box '''DynamicViscosity'''.
::A measure of a fluids resistance to deformation at a given rate and sometimes referred to as the absolute viscosity. The dynamic viscosity divided by the fluid's density gives the kinematic viscosity.
</div>

* Nicht-isotherm

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
:* Optionally select '''Compressible'''.
:: When a non-isothermal simulation is selected, in the [[CfdOF_PhysicsModel|Physics Model]], the '''Compressible''' checkbox becomes visible. If unchecked, incompressible fluids can be simulated within a compressible solver, if the compressibility plays no significant role.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
:* Input box '''Molar Mass'''.
::The Molar Mass or Molecular Weight of the fluid.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
:*'''Polynomials'''
::Temperature-polynomials are used when simulating non-isothermal, incompressible fluids. The simulation occurs over a temperature range and the fluid property data must be determined over the range. 
::The Polynomial provides a means of inputting coefficients for a temperature-polynomial, in the format:
:::Temperature-polynomial  = a + b × T + c × T<sup>2</sup> + d × T <sup>3</sup>
::where a, b, c and d are coefficients and T is Temperature. The polynomials will accept up to 8 coefficients. Enter each coefficient, separated by a space, for the temperature-polynomial, starting with the constant followed by the higher powers.
:::e.g. Cp = 9850.69 -48.6714xT +0.13736T<sup>2</sup> -0.000127063xT<sup>3</sup> 
::would be entered as:
:::9850.69 -48.6714 0.13736 -0.000127063
</div>    

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
::* Input box '''CpPolynomial'''.
::: Temperature-polynomial for Specific Heat Capacity at constant pressure.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
::* Input box '''DensityPolynomial'''.
:::Temperature-polynomial for Density.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
::* Input box '''DynamicViscosityPolynomial'''.
:::Temperature-polynomial for Dynamic Viscosity.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
::* Input box '''ThermalConductivityPolynomial'''.
:::Temperature-polynomial for Thermal Conductivity.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
:*'''Sutherland'''
::The Sutherland formula is used when simulating non-isothermal, compressible fluids. It is used for approximating the viscosity of gases, at different temperatures. It is used for single-component gases at low pressure, however can also be used for Air, as the main components of Nitrogen and Oxygen have very similar properties.
</div>   

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
::* Input box '''SutherlandRefTemperature'''.
:::The temperature, T<sub>0</sub>,  at which the '''SutherlandRefViscosity''' was determined.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
::* Input box '''SutherlandRefViscosity'''.
:::The empirical reference viscosity, μ<sub>0</sub>, determined at the '''SutherlandRefTemperature'''.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
::* Input box '''SutherlandTemperature'''.
:::Sometimes referred to as the Sutherland constant, S, is an effective temperature, which is characteristic of the gas.
</div> 

<span id="Predefined_fluid_library"></span>
===Vordefinierte Flüssigkeitsbibliothek===

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
* Drop-down box
: The fluid properties for a number of components exist within the '''Predefined fluid library'''. Selecting a fluid from the drop-down, will automatically populate the '''Fluid properties''' input boxes above
</div>  

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
*Reference data
: Below the drop-down, the reference conditions for the fluid selected are given. e.g. for incompressible Seawater it states "Sea water (30%) 20 Degrees Celsius 1 atm".
</div>   

<span id="Notes"></span>
==Hinweise==

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
* The input boxes and options available in the Fluid Properties task panel are dependent on the options selected in the [[CfdOF_PhysicsModel|Physics Model]] task panel. To assist with workflow, we recommend that you complete the [[CfdOF_PhysicsModel|Physics Model]] task panel before completing this task panel.
</div> 


